@prefix tgs:   <http://example.org/tgs#> .
@prefix thinkr: <http://example.org/thinkr#> .
@prefix owl:   <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs:  <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:  <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dct:   <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix dcmitype: <http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/> .
@prefix xsd:   <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

#############################################
# episodes-tgs-1-25.ttl -- PILOT #2, 2026-09-12.
# TGS interview episodes numbered 1 through 25, split out of
# episodes.ttl as the second real pilot for the TGS/RR/Frankly
# three-way split -- the first, more complex test of stub-minting for
# genuinely unbuilt episodes (RR's own pilot needed none, being a
# fully complete set).
#
# 19 of 25 numbers were already fully built. 6 were real, confirmed
# gaps at the start of this pilot (11, 13, 16, 21, 23, 25) -- though
# #11 (Rex Weyler) was corrected mid-build: this session's own earlier
# site-inventory scrape had silently missed it entirely, since its
# real URL slug (episode/rex-weyler) carries no leading number, unlike
# virtually every other episode on the site. Given how much real,
# confirmed detail was available for #11 via search, it received a
# real, direct light build rather than a bare stub; #13, #16, #21,
# #23, #25 remain genuine minimal stubs (real number/title/URL only).
#
# Sorted by episode number. tgs:Series.BendNotBreak grouped at the top
# per the standing convention, since 2 of its real 5 members (TGS-5,
# TGS-20) fall within this range -- the other 3 (TGS-31, 42, 50) do
# not yet live in this file, a real, legitimate cross-file reference
# rather than an error.
#
# episodes.ttl itself is left completely untouched by this pilot.
#############################################

<http://example.org/tgs#> a owl:Ontology ;
    owl:imports <http://example.org/thinkr#> ;
    rdfs:comment "Imports tgs-core for Protege compatibility — editing this file alone will resolve thinkr: classes/properties via the catalog file."@en .

tgs:Series.BendNotBreak a thinkr:Series, dcmitype:Collection, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dct:hasPart
        tgs:Interview.TGS_5_BendNotBreakPart1,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_20_MaximumPowerHyperAgents,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_31_SensemakingUncertaintyPurpose,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_42_ModelingDriversMetacrisis,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_50_CriteriaCategoriesResponse ;
    skos:prefLabel "Bend Not Break"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Discovered 2026-07-13 while verifying 3 previously-unrelated Schmachtenberger episode entries — turned out to be a real, distinct 5-part series never before modeled. Confirmed via Nate's own words (a Substack post about Part 3) that the series wasn't originally planned as 5 parts — it grew organically from conversation, first to 4 planned parts, then 5 once Part 3 diverged from its intended topic."@en ;
    .

tgs:Interview.TGS_1_RichardGephardt a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    skos:prefLabel "Richard Gephardt: Democracy, Social Media, and the Case for Public Service"@en ;
    skos:definition "The show's real first-ever episode. Nate hosts former US House Majority/Minority Leader Richard Gephardt — a real, direct professional collaborator on a project called 'advance policy,' not just a first-time guest — for Gephardt's own real first-ever podcast appearance of any kind. Covers the real conflict between social-media business models and functioning democracy, a direct real contrast between the national unity following 9/11 and the division surrounding January 6th, and Gephardt's own real, extensive account of his path from a working-class St. Louis upbringing into public service."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.RichardGephardt ;
    dct:created "2021-12-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/01-dickgephardt> ;
    dct:references tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct date inconsistency in the source material, flagged honestly rather than silently resolved: the show notes list 'Episode 1... Dec 9, 2019' as the issued date, but 'Recorded on: Dec 17, 2021' — an issued date nearly 2 years BEFORE the stated recording date, which is not just unusual but chronologically impossible. This is almost certainly a real site/metadata error, not a genuine fact — further supported by comparison: tgs:Interview.TGS_15_DanielPauly (Episode 15, already built) issued April 2022, only ~2 months after its own Feb 2022 recording — a real, consistent pattern this episode's stated 2019 issue date would badly violate for only 14 episodes' difference. dct:issued deliberately left unasserted here rather than guessing between two implausible options; dct:created (the real recording date) is asserted with confidence. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Tristan Harris and Daniel Schmachtenberger's real joint appearance on the Joe Rogan podcast, directly cited (Schmachtenberger already a real, built Persona in this graph); Tom Downey, directly named as 'a mutual colleague' of Nate and Gephardt, real but not independently confirmed or built in this pass."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A genuinely personal episode from its opening minutes — Nate's first real question isn't about policy but about why an 80-year-old with a storied career isn't simply retired, and Gephardt's own answer is a real, extended account of his working-class St. Louis childhood: a father whose back injury ended his trucking career, a mother who went to work, and a real, specific formative moment — a high school speech teacher personally arranging a Northwestern summer scholarship that Gephardt directly credits as opening his entire adult path. The episode's real analytical core is Gephardt's own direct comparison between the 9/11 aftermath and January 6th: he offers a real, personal account of running through a small Missouri town on the Christmas after 9/11 and finding every house flying an American flag in solidarity with New York, offered as concrete evidence of genuine national unity — directly contrasted with January 6th, which he attributes to Americans who had been, in his own words, effectively 'brainwashed' by an alternative-reality information ecosystem into believing violence was justified. Gephardt is careful throughout to extend real empathy alongside his critique, stating directly that he understands the mindset that produced January 6th even while condemning the outcome. The episode's closing stretch turns genuinely personal and practical: Gephardt's own real daily routine (two meals within an eight-hour window, a standing bowl of raw vegetables) is directly credited by Nate as something he adopted into his own life as a result of this conversation. The episode closes with real, warm testimony to Gephardt's own reputation — Nate directly relays that mutual colleagues refer to him, half-joking, as 'a saint and a national treasure and a superman' — and Gephardt's own closing words return to a real, concrete personal ethic borrowed from his time as a Boy Scout: leave the campsite better than you found it."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_2_ShannaSwan a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    skos:prefLabel "Shanna Swan: Sperm Counts, Endocrine Disruption, and the Future of Human Reproduction"@en ;
    skos:definition "Swan's real first-ever TGS appearance — one of the podcast's earliest episodes. Nate hosts reproductive epidemiologist Shanna Swan on the real, direct thesis of her then-new 2021 book 'Countdown': endocrine-disrupting chemicals in the environment are driving measurable declines in human sperm count and fertility, with real average sperm concentration falling from roughly 99 to 47 million per milliliter by the 2011 measurement she cites. Covers phthalates, real historical parallels (Tyrone Hayes' atrazine/alligator research at Lake Apopka), and national policy responses — Denmark's fertility campaigns contrasted directly with Israel's real, fully state-funded assisted-reproduction program."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ShannaSwan ;
    dct:created "2021-11-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-01-07"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/02-shannaswan> ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_23_ThePlasticDetox ;
    skos:scopeNote "Resolves a real, repeatedly-flagged deferred reference: both RR-23 and TGS_99 (Jeremy Grantham) directly cited this real episode without it being built until now — both scopeNotes updated in the same pass. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Tyrone Hayes, directly named for his real atrazine/alligator research, now built (2026-09-02)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A genuinely foundational episode for a real, recurring throughline across this whole graph — this is the real primary source for content directly cited years later in RR-23, RR-15, and TGS_99 (Jeremy Grantham's own direct funding of Swan's research). Swan is careful to preempt an obvious objection early: since conception only requires one sperm, why does a falling average count matter? Her real, direct answer treats sperm count as a distribution, not a threshold — quality and viability requirements mean a large surplus is needed to reliably produce even one successful candidate, and average counts below roughly 40 million per milliliter are clinically considered subfertile. A real, striking comparative policy discussion follows: Denmark's approach, which pairs fertility incentives with real, sustained public attention to environmental exposures, is credited by Swan with actually halting decline in long-term study data — directly contrasted with several Asian countries she names as offering only financial incentives without addressing exposure, which she states plainly 'is not working.' Nate offers a real, speculative extension live in conversation, drawing a direct parallel between a hypothetical future where only a small fraction of fertile men father most children and the real historical case of Genghis Khan's Y-chromosome persisting in a notable percentage of East Asian and, by some estimates, American men today — offered explicitly as speculation, not as something Swan endorses. The episode closes with Swan's own real, direct appeal: she wants this issue to generate the same public 'buzz' climate change has achieved, stating it may be 'perhaps even as serious as that and deeply connected to it' — a real, direct claim about relative severity, not just topical adjacency."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_3_OilBestAndWorstOfFuels a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Oil: It Was the Best of Fuels, It Was the Worst of Fuels"@en ;
    dct:created "2021-12-29"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-01-07"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ArtBerman ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/03-arthurberman> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.PeakOil ;
    skos:scopeNote "2026-08-13: real dates confirmed and discusses links added from a complete real transcript + show notes upload (Nate's own first-ever TGS interview, per MJSullivan) — previously a bare stub with no confirmed date. This is Nate and Berman's very first recorded conversation together, explicitly framed by Nate as a primer/introduction to oil basics rather than an advanced deep-dive."@en .

tgs:Interview.TGS_4_ChuckWatson a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Chuck Watson: \"From MAD to NUTS: Risk, Nukes, & Climate Change\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct first conversation with his own real, longstanding friend Chuck Watson, whom he'd known for roughly 15 years by this point through Watson's own real hurricane-damage infrastructure maps used on The Oil Drum. Watson directly introduces his own real, coined distinction between MAD (Mutually Assured Destruction, a real, stable deterrence posture) and NUTS (Nuclear Utilization Targeting Strategy, a real, more dangerous doctrine treating nuclear weapons as potentially usable rather than purely deterrent) — arguing the world's drift from the former toward the latter is a genuinely underappreciated risk."@en ;
    dct:created "2021-12-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-01-26"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ChuckWatson ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/04-chuckwatson> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=t_MfMSPaLBc> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/TGS04ChuckWatsonTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Watson directly introduces 'risk homeostasis' — a real, established behavioral concept he applies throughout his later appearances too — describing how humans unconsciously recalibrate their own risk tolerance based on recent experience rather than actual underlying danger, making repeated near-misses (rather than actual disasters) a genuine driver of complacency. He's real and direct about a striking real academic estimate: some published risk analyses put the cumulative chance of nuclear war over the preceding 50 years at roughly 60%, framing humanity's continued survival as real, significant luck rather than reliably managed safety. A real, direct historical case anchors his concern about institutional erosion: the Able Archer 83 incident, a real, documented 1983 NATO exercise that nearly triggered an actual Soviet nuclear response due to genuine mutual misperception, offered directly as evidence that even well-intentioned actors can stumble into catastrophe through simple miscalculation. Watson directly connects nuclear risk to this graph's own broader themes: arguing humans are cognitively mismatched for evaluating genuinely low-probability, high-consequence risks, the same underlying mismatch driving climate inaction. Closes on real, direct, practical advice: press political leaders to consider international, not just domestic, consequences of policy choices — using real, current US natural gas exports to Europe as a direct example of a policy serving domestic economic interests while potentially destabilizing international relationships."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, valuable cross-reference surfaced directly in this episode's own real show notes: Dick Gephardt's real, direct first TGS appearance (#1, 'Dick Gephardt on the Iraq War') — distinct from his real, later appearance (#148, already flagged elsewhere in this graph's own tracking as 'Richard Gephardt: Preserving Democracy'). Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely encyclopedic/institutional (MAD, NUTS, hypersonic weapons) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    .


