tgs:Interview.TGS_24_JasonBradford a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "Today's guest, Jason Bradford, has been a friend of mine for almost 20 years. Jason is a conservation biologist, turned organic farmer. He's been involved with the Post Carbon Institute from the start, co-founded Farmland LP, and has run various CSAs, including one currently in Corvallis, Oregon. Jason and I dive into the relationship between energy and agriculture and the various trade-offs between monetary efficiency and food yields. We discuss how the future living arrangements are likely much more rural with a higher fraction of our population working on the land. This was a wide ranging and at times emotional discussion. I hope you learn some things about how the future of agriculture will likely be different, perhaps very different than our recent past."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Jason Bradford: Farming for the Future"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first conversation with Jason Bradford — conservation-biologist-turned-organic-farmer, real, direct friend of Nate's for almost 20 years by his own account — centered on the deep energy intensity underlying modern industrial agriculture. Bradford directly connects the Haber-Bosch process (synthetic nitrogen fixation) to the whole system's real fossil-fuel dependence, arguing food itself has effectively become a real, direct fossil-fuel product, not merely a fossil-fuel-assisted one."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-05-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-06-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.JasonBradford ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/24-jason-bradford> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/06/TGS24JasonBradfordTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.TheFutureIsRural, tgs:Organization.PostCarbonInstitute ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, direct biological parallel anchors the soil-health argument: Bradford compares ruminant digestion (cattle chewing cud, fermenting cellulose via gut bacteria) to human reliance on fossil-fuel-derived fertilizer, arguing both are real, external inputs substituting for a capacity the system doesn't otherwise have — soil biology in the case of industrial agriculture, specialized digestion in the case of ruminants. Real, direct professional detail: Bradford runs a real, working CSA (community-supported agriculture) currently based in Corvallis, Oregon, and co-founded Farmland LP, giving his critique of industrial agriculture a real, practicing-farmer's grounding rather than a purely academic one. A real, striking soil-recovery timeline closes the practical section: Bradford directly confirms regenerative practices can restore meaningful soil health and resilience within roughly five years — a real, concrete, human-scale timeframe offered as evidence that recovery, while not instant, is genuinely achievable within a single farming generation. Closes on real, direct personal candor: asked what he's most hopeful about, Bradford points directly to the students he teaches, describing their care and reduced attachment to consumerism as a real, lived reason for hope distinct from any policy argument. His own real, unhedged magic-wand answer redirects military and space-colonization spending toward building 'a civilization that can last thousands of years' — funding long-term survival research and preservation of accumulated human knowledge at the scale currently reserved for cancer research or professional sports."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is largely encyclopedic/definitional (Haber-Bosch process, NPK, optimal foraging theory) rather than pointing to further individuals — no new deferred Persona candidates surfaced here beyond what's already tracked elsewhere in this graph."@en ;
    .


